A table can build the answer from short rods to long rods. For each target length, every possible last piece is tested. Since a piece can be used more than once, the already computed value for the remaining length is enough.

A small dry run for `n = 4` checks values for lengths 1, 2, 3, and 4 in order. When length 4 is reached, all smaller answers are ready, so every possible cut can be combined safely.

```cpp
class Solution{
  public:
    int rodCutting(vector<int> price, int n) {
      vector<int> best(n + 1, 0);

      for (int length = 1; length <= n; length++) {
        for (int piece = 1; piece <= length; piece++) {
          best[length] = max(best[length],
                             price[piece - 1] + best[length - piece]);
        }
      }

      return best[n];
    }
};
```

The running time is `O(n^2)` and the table takes `O(n)` space. The passed vector is copied by the platform signature, and the code does not modify it.

This keeps a table where `dp[pieceCount][length]` means the best price for a rod of that length when only pieces up to `pieceCount` are allowed. At each step, the current piece is either skipped or used once more.

A dry run for piece length 2 and rod length 4 can use that piece twice. That is why the take case stays on the same row instead of moving to the previous row.

```java
class Solution{
    public int RodCutting(int price[], int n) {
        int[][] dp = new int[n + 1][n + 1];

        for (int piece = 1; piece <= n; piece++) {
            for (int length = 1; length <= n; length++) {
                dp[piece][length] = dp[piece - 1][length];

                if (piece <= length) {
                    dp[piece][length] = Math.max(
                            dp[piece][length],
                            price[piece - 1] + dp[piece][length - piece]);
                }
            }
        }

        return dp[n][n];
    }
}
```

Both the time and space costs are `O(n^2)`. This version uses more memory than a one-dimensional table, but the two choices, skip or take, are more visible. A rod of length zero naturally keeps the default value zero.

The recursive choices become much faster when the answer for each rod length is stored. Once `best[length]` has been calculated, later calls reuse it instead of cutting the same length again.

The price at index `cut - 1` is used for a piece of length `cut`. The input array is not changed.

```java
class Solution{
    public int RodCutting(int price[], int n) {
        int[] best = new int[n + 1];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(best, -1);
        return findBest(price, n, best);
    }

    private int findBest(int[] price, int length, int[] best) {
        if (length == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        if (best[length] != -1) {
            return best[length];
        }

        int answer = 0;
        for (int cut = 1; cut <= length; cut++) {
            answer = Math.max(answer,
                    price[cut - 1] + findBest(price, length - cut, best));
        }

        best[length] = answer;
        return answer;
    }
}
```

The time complexity is `O(n^2)`, since each length tries all possible first cuts. The extra space is `O(n)` for the stored answers and recursion stack. If `n` is zero, the method returns zero without reading the price array.

Here the table is filled by choosing the first piece of each possible length. The remaining part is read from the table, so repeated pieces are allowed and no special case is needed for them.

The edge case `n = 0` returns zero because the table contains `dp[0]`. This assumes `price` has an entry for every length from 1 through `n`, as described by the function signature.

```go
func RodCutting(price []int, n int) int {
	if n <= 0 {
		return 0
	}

	dp := make([]int, n+1)

	for length := 1; length <= n; length++ {
		best := 0
		for firstCut := 1; firstCut <= length; firstCut++ {
			candidate := price[firstCut-1] + dp[length-firstCut]
			if candidate > best {
				best = candidate
			}
		}
		dp[length] = best
	}

	return dp[n]
}
```

There are `n` lengths and up to `n` cuts for each one, giving `O(n^2)` time. The array uses `O(n)` extra space. Negative prices are also handled because the initial best value for each length is zero, which represents making no profitable cut.

